      "text": "It worked over the past year, but with only 3 trades that's weak evidence that it will keep working.\n\n**Backtest: QQQ, 14-day RSI, buy under 30 / sell over 70, Sep 26 2025 \u2013 Sep 25 2026, $10k starting capital**\n\n| | RSI 30/70 | Buy & hold QQQ |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total return | **+27.8%** | +25.5% ($593.18 \u2192 $744.50) |\n| Max drawdown | -2.4% | not calculated |\n| Sharpe ratio | 3.56 | \u2014 |\n| Trades | 3 (all winners) | \u2014 |\n\n**The three trades:**\n1. Nov 18 \u2192 Dec 9, 2025: $594.11 \u2192 $621.50, **+4.4%**\n2. Mar 27 \u2192 Apr 14, 2026: $561.94 \u2192 $626.63, **+11.3%**\n3. Jul 29 \u2192 Aug 14, 2026: $661.70 \u2192 $729.58, **+10.0%**\n\n**What stands out:**\n- **It barely beat buy-and-hold, with much less time in the market.** The strategy held QQQ for only about 37 of ~250 trading days (around 15%) and sat in cash the rest of the time. It got a slightly higher return with much less exposure to the market. That's why the drawdown and Sharpe look so good.\n- **Three trades don't prove much.** The 100% win rate and 3.56 Sharpe come from three dips that each bounced quickly. One sharp drop that kept falling after RSI went under 30 would have looked very different. The strategy has no stop-loss, so it would have held all the way down.\n- **Last year was friendly to this approach.** QQQ rose steadily with short, sharp pullbacks, which is exactly when buying oversold dips works. In a long bear market like 2022, RSI can stay under 30 for weeks and \"buying the dip\" means catching a falling knife.\n- **The tool's own summary contradicts itself on one point.** It lists \"unprofitable trades overall\" as a weakness, even though all three trades made money.\n\n**Bottom line:** In this one-year window it slightly beat buy-and-hold with far less risk. But three trades in a rising market isn't enough to trust it. Next, I can run a walk-forward test, a Monte Carlo simulation, or a parameter optimization (such as 35/65 thresholds for more signals) to see whether the result holds up."
